so i want to start today with one of the  two big areas where there is  a particularly high amount of  disinformation spreading right now now  earlier in the week we talked about one  such area and that's coveted in vaccines  the i don't even know what to call it  it's almost like colony calling it the  launch seems weird it's almost like a  product but the breakthrough of the  omicron variant into the news which  started on the day after thanksgiving  last week and led to  two very very shaky days in the stock  market that's one topic that has lent  itself to significant disinformation  right-wing media and republicans going  crazy with false claims about the  variant itself false claims about covid  false claims about vaccines etc etc we  covered that monday we covered it to  some extent yesterday tuesday the other  area that is  being victimized by disinformation from  the right at this point in time  is the economy  and by the economy that includes the  supply chain stuff  employment  inflation  and a few other areas that's another  area of significant disinformation with  such  esteemed talking points from the right  as  inflation is never going to end and it's  going to destroy the economy  nobody wants to work anymore because of  unemployment and there's no workers and  the job situation is a disaster  etc etc you guys have heard a lot of  these uh talking points oh and also  there's no uh chinese-made tchotchkes  for christmas on the shelves thanks to  joe biden okay so those are the kind of  general topics  when we actually look  at the economic data  that is generally accepted as signaling  how the economy is doing now i'll get  back to that because that's not a  trivial matter there are measures we  generally accept to tell us about the  economy we could say these are the wrong  measures but we're going to get back to  that when you look at those measures  things actually look okay  um a few  new as we are now into december a few  new bullet points of data that were  released today  initial  jobless claims meaning new unemployment  claims  fell to their lowest level  last month november since november of  1960  on what's called the seasonally adjusted  basis okay that's the the week before  thanksgiving  um now unadjusted numbers increased and  this is an important thing to understand  there were 199 000 new unemployment  claims during the week ending november  20th the week before thanksgiving  now those have been what's called  seasonally adjusted this is an accepted  method for getting what we might call a  truer  number  which controls for the layoff patterns  that are expected based on the time of  year that it is there are  industries including retail  agriculture and others  which have seasonal patterns for hiring  and laying people off and they don't  really represent  a true hire or layoff in some sense so  there's this process called adjustment  now if you don't adjust the numbers  claims actually did go up a little bit  by 18 000 to almost 259 000 that week  before thanksgiving but the general  picture  is that these are very low numbers for  new unemployment claims so that's one  side of the equation it actually looks  pretty good  um the labor department actually  expected many more people to apply for  unemployment benefits for the first time  in that week before thanksgiving they  anticipated 70 000 more it was actually  only 18 000 more and that's a good thing  by this metric okay  now there's a question as to whether you  know i said the lowest in 52 years  we have changed a number of things over  that 52-year period and so there's a  legitimate question as to are we really  comparing apples to apples when we  compare 2021 to 1969 or some period in  between because there have been changes  in that interim period for example some  states have made it harder to get  unemployment particularly since the  great recession of 2008-2009  so that would reduce the number who even  apply because they know it's harder to  actually get the benefits that's that's  that's fair that could be a factor  on the other hand  since the start of the pandemic more  people are aware that such benefits  could be made available to them so that  would be a factor that compared to the  70s and 80s for example makes more  people apply generally speaking you take  a very top-line look new unemployment  claims are very very low on an  unadjusted basis on an adjusted basis  dating back uh five decades  really pieces of six decades all the way  back to 1969. so that's one thing what  about job creation on the other side of  unemployment is job creation well  private payrolls went up  by 534 000 in november  that's quite a bit better than the dow  jones expectation of 506 000 new jobs  expected was 506 actual was 534.  um leisure and hospitality saw again  um companies with more than 500  employees saw a big gain companies with  over a thousand employees saw an even  bigger proportional gain  and so that's all pretty good by the  standards that we use so the question  marks now are  what happens with omicron and to what  degree does that impact the economy at  least in the us and this is an economic  statement i'm making not a public health  statement there does not seem to be much  if any appetite for anything that would  approximate a shutdown or a lockdown of  any kind  now whether it will make sense from a  public health standpoint first we need  to know how serious omicron is we still  don't  but that's a public health question  dealing with this as an economic  question it doesn't seem like there's  really much appetite for that what  happens with supply chain um and are  these even good metrics and that's  something that is a different question  you know there are systemic problems  with the economy that i talk about all  the time that i know many of you are  aware of which aren't part of this  analysis when we look at new  unemployment claims or job creation or  whatever we aren't talking about wage  growth we're not talking about the  reality that the minimum wage in most of  the united states  is not a living wage as defined by can  you even get a basic two-bedroom  apartment in the place where you are  employed at the minimum wage  those are systemic problems that still  exist and there's no question that the  metrics we use fall woefully short that  being said  the right accepts these metrics and so  on their own terms it's very difficult  to argue that we have a bad economy  right now we haven't solved not  everybody has health care  no that doesn't change but by the  standards the right accepts things are  actually looking okay and it's important  to understand that when you hear their  despairing talking points on fox news  and elsewhere  one of our sponsors is magic spoon  breakfast cereal sweet and tasty like  the cereals i loved as a kid but without  the sugar or the carbs every serving of  magic spoon has zero grams of sugar only  four net carbs 140 calories and packed  with 13 grams of protein perfect if  you're doing keto perfect if you're  doing low carb great if you're just  trying to reduce your excess sugar  intake and because it's packed with  protein it's great before or after a  workout magic spoon flavors include  cocoa fruity frosted peanut butter  blueberry and cinnamon they're bringing  back cookies and cream they're bringing  back maple waffle you can build your own  variety box and try them all magic spoon  is so confident in the product if you  don't love it as much as i do they will  refund your money no questions asked go  to magicspoon.com pacman the link is  underneath this video and you'll get  five dollars off when you use the promo  code pacman at checkout
 

